{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And the firstborn of a donkey.</b> And not [the firstborn of] any other unclean animal." "<b>You shall redeem with a lamb.</b> He gives a lamb to the priest, and it is ḥullin in the priest's hand, and the firstborn of a donkey is permitted for labor to its owners." "<b>And you shall break its neck.</b> He breaks its neck with a cleaver. He deprived the priest of money — therefore his money shall be deprived." "<b>All the firstborn of your sons you shall redeem.</b> Five shekels is its redemption fixed, as it says ״וּפְדוּיָו מִבֶּן חֹדֶשׁ תִּפְדֶה״ (Numbers 18)." "<b>And they shall not see My face empty.</b> According to the plain sense of Scripture, this is a matter on its own — it is not suspended over the firstborn, for there is no requirement of 'seeing the face' in the law of the firstborn. Rather, it is another warning: when you go up on the pilgrimage festival to see, they shall not see My face empty — it is a commandment upon you to bring an olah of 'seeing the face.' And according to the midrash of the Baraita, it is an extra verse, set free for a gezerah shavah, to teach about the farewell-gift of a Hebrew slave that it is five shekels of every kind, like the redemption of the firstborn — in tractate Kiddushin (page 17)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:34.901374508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}